WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:00.640
SAAD ALI: All right.

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:01.300
Hello, everyone.

00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:02.133
My name is Saad Ali.

00:00:02.133 --> 00:00:05.370
We're here at KubeCon
Barcelona, and with me today

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:07.170
is Aaron Crickenberger.

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:09.690
He has a lot of
hats that he wears.

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:11.220
He's a Google engineer.

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:13.770
He is on the Kubernetes
Steering Committee,

00:00:13.770 --> 00:00:17.550
and he's been involved with a
lot about Kubernetes releases.

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:19.950
Aaron, can you talk a little
bit about the releases

00:00:19.950 --> 00:00:21.720
that you've been
involved with and how

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:22.950
they've evolved over time?

00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:23.520
AARON CRICKENBERGER: Sure.

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:25.312
I've been involved with
Kubernetes releases

00:00:25.312 --> 00:00:28.770
since Kubernetes was version
1.4, and at the beginning,

00:00:28.770 --> 00:00:30.510
it was just sort of
a select few Google

00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:33.540
people who were hardcore enough
and had the right privileges

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:34.990
to make this happen.

00:00:34.990 --> 00:00:37.960
Over time, I saw the release
process gradually hand

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:41.280
over more and more
control to the community.

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:43.230
I started out writing
release notes,

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:45.540
you know, attempting to
sort of reverse engineer

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:49.680
a coherent narrative out of this
chaotic, open-source process.

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:53.940
And over time, grew into other
roles such as issue triage--

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:56.100
keeping track of
how do we actually

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:57.930
know what's happening
within the release,

00:00:57.930 --> 00:01:00.180
and make sure the right
things are being focused on--

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:02.040
to CI signal-- you
know, how do we

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:04.980
make sure that a given
Kubernetes release is actually

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:07.500
healthy and ready to
ship out the door?

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:10.710
And that sort of culminated
in me acting as a release

00:01:10.710 --> 00:01:14.380
lead for Kubernetes 1.14, which
I was especially proud of,

00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:18.390
because it was the release with
the most stable enhancements

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:19.700
out of any Kubernetes release.

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:21.075
And it was also
the first release

00:01:21.075 --> 00:01:24.870
where the community agreed to
use the Kubernetes Enhancement

00:01:24.870 --> 00:01:27.700
Proposal process,
or KEP process,

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:30.900
to ship features,
which to me, was really

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:35.170
a sign of just how mature
the release process has come.

00:01:35.170 --> 00:01:36.690
And while I was
doing this, I was

00:01:36.690 --> 00:01:40.050
assisted by a team
of over 40 people

00:01:40.050 --> 00:01:43.050
serving in 13 different
roles to ensure

00:01:43.050 --> 00:01:46.440
that mostly all I had to
do was watch everything

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:48.270
proceed super smoothly.

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:53.370
And it culminated in me getting
to create this cat T-shirt here

00:01:53.370 --> 00:01:54.960
where every release
team member gets

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:58.740
a piece of swag as sort of
the honorary badge of running

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:01.230
the release for three months.

00:02:01.230 --> 00:02:04.170
And my piece of swag
was cat-ernetes,

00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:07.680
you know, three cats looking
up at the Kubernetes logo

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:09.810
sort of like three wolf moon.

00:02:09.810 --> 00:02:10.889
SAAD ALI: Very cool.

00:02:10.889 --> 00:02:13.560
And so you mentioned how this
process has become better

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:16.000
and is more community owned.

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:17.580
There's a lot of
other work going on

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:19.870
to improve the
Kubernetes community.

00:02:19.870 --> 00:02:21.657
Can you talk about some of that?

00:02:21.657 --> 00:02:22.740
AARON CRICKENBERGER: Sure.

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:24.690
Back when Kubernetes
first started,

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:27.008
we weren't even sort
of very organized

00:02:27.008 --> 00:02:28.300
in terms of what we were doing.

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:30.750
It was just some
very driven companies

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:32.610
and contributors
trying to push code out

00:02:32.610 --> 00:02:34.175
as quickly as possible.

00:02:34.175 --> 00:02:35.550
And we gradually
realized that we

00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:37.290
needed to get like-minded
people together

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:39.240
to talk about different
areas that they were

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:41.520
interested in coordinating on.

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:45.390
So I used to work with a
guy named Bob Weiss who--

00:02:45.390 --> 00:02:47.790
I worked with him to
co-found SIG Scalability,

00:02:47.790 --> 00:02:49.890
the first special
interest group.

00:02:49.890 --> 00:02:52.740
I then worked to co-found
the second special interest

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:55.800
group called SIG Testing, all
about the testing of Kubernetes

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:57.360
to make sure that worked well.

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:00.780
Today, we now have over 31
special interest groups,

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:02.520
and we've also
created working groups

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:05.568
that are collaborations between
special interest groups.

00:03:05.568 --> 00:03:07.110
So we've really sort
of federated out

00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:09.150
and empowered the
decision-making process

00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:11.100
for this open-source community.

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:12.840
And at the same
time, we've been very

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:16.260
intentional about focusing
on inclusion and diversity

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:19.650
through this whole process,
taking a look at how we engage

00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:22.830
with our community members
and with our contributors,

00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:24.450
and looking at the
examples that we

00:03:24.450 --> 00:03:28.740
set to ensure that we're acting
in as inclusive a manner as

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:32.640
possible, sort of setting
up work and describing roles

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:35.460
to make it easier to
onboard people who are maybe

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:37.560
intimidated or overwhelmed
by contributing

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:42.900
to a project that is as large
and fast moving as Kubernetes.

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:47.450
So I've really been happy
to see that sort of pay off.

00:03:47.450 --> 00:03:49.290
And I think that attitude
has been reflected

00:03:49.290 --> 00:03:53.400
in the KubeCon conference that
sort of hangs around Kubernetes

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:57.180
where there's these diversity
scholarships that happen

00:03:57.180 --> 00:04:00.510
each year, and you sort of see
this nice exponential growth

00:04:00.510 --> 00:04:01.830
curve.

00:04:01.830 --> 00:04:05.530
I thought it was really neat to
hear the story today of Nikita

00:04:05.530 --> 00:04:09.810
[INAUDIBLE],, who described her
journey from sort of college

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:13.980
student to owner of
very fundamental parts

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:16.140
of the Kubernetes
code base today.

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:18.660
And, you know, she got
there by showing up

00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:21.329
to one of these special
interest groups,

00:04:21.329 --> 00:04:23.640
feeling like she was heard.

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:26.640
She found a mentor who was
willing to help her out,

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:30.390
and she managed to get
to her first KubeCon

00:04:30.390 --> 00:04:32.280
by way of a
diversity scholarship

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:35.820
to really experience
all the wonderful stuff

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:38.440
that this community
has to offer.

00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:40.170
I also thought another
really great thing

00:04:40.170 --> 00:04:44.120
I saw in this KubeCon was
sort of more of a focus

00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:47.520
on mental health,
realizing that because this

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:50.550
is such a large and
overwhelming open-source

00:04:50.550 --> 00:04:56.190
project that some people
can maybe burn out or maybe

00:04:56.190 --> 00:04:58.780
feel a little uncertain
of themselves.

00:04:58.780 --> 00:05:01.640
And so I actually tweeted
about this at the last KubeCon.

00:05:01.640 --> 00:05:04.040
It was super exhausting
to me, actually.

00:05:04.040 --> 00:05:06.230
I think I gave six
talks throughout a week.

00:05:06.230 --> 00:05:07.140
It was a lot.

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:08.720
And I said, hey,
I think we really

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:10.370
need to talk about burnout.

00:05:10.370 --> 00:05:13.610
And I heard back
from over 200 people

00:05:13.610 --> 00:05:15.530
sharing with me
their experiences

00:05:15.530 --> 00:05:17.270
and what they were
up to and what

00:05:17.270 --> 00:05:18.510
they were coming up against.

00:05:18.510 --> 00:05:20.810
And some of the
constructive criticism

00:05:20.810 --> 00:05:23.750
that they had to offer both
me as an individual, as well

00:05:23.750 --> 00:05:26.900
as the conference
organizers, to improve this.

00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:30.970
And today, I just came out
of a mental health talk

00:05:30.970 --> 00:05:36.440
to hear how companies
who contribute

00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:40.350
to all of the projects here
that are hosted by the CNCF

00:05:40.350 --> 00:05:42.968
are taking mental
health very seriously.

00:05:42.968 --> 00:05:44.010
SAAD ALI: That's awesome.

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:46.552
So it sounds like there's a lot
of really great work going on

00:05:46.552 --> 00:05:49.640
to make the Kubernetes community
not only more diverse, but also

00:05:49.640 --> 00:05:51.300
more sustainable.

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:53.943
Make sure that we can continue
to do all the great work

00:05:53.943 --> 00:05:54.860
that we've been doing.

00:05:54.860 --> 00:05:57.017
So thank you for all
your work on that.

00:05:57.017 --> 00:05:58.850
Last question-- what
are you looking forward

00:05:58.850 --> 00:06:02.318
to here at KubeCon this year?

00:06:02.318 --> 00:06:04.360
AARON CRICKENBERGER: Well,
like I said earlier, I

00:06:04.360 --> 00:06:06.235
think the thing I was
most looking forward to

00:06:06.235 --> 00:06:10.440
was getting to see that mental
health talk and hear the--

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:13.520
like, I stayed after
the talk and heard

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:16.310
so many community members
ask questions and follow-up

00:06:16.310 --> 00:06:17.360
questions afterwards.

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:20.600
That was my second talk was just
hearing everybody else engage

00:06:20.600 --> 00:06:22.780
and following that conversation.

00:06:22.780 --> 00:06:25.160
I'm also, selfishly,
really interested

00:06:25.160 --> 00:06:30.170
in my panel at the end of
Thursday to talk about burnout

00:06:30.170 --> 00:06:31.940
and how we can
leverage this community

00:06:31.940 --> 00:06:36.005
to set better norms for self
care and things like that.

00:06:36.005 --> 00:06:36.630
SAAD ALI: Cool.

00:06:36.630 --> 00:06:38.000
Well, thank you so much
for your time, Aaron.

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:38.510
AARON CRICKENBERGER: Cool.

00:06:38.510 --> 00:06:39.572
Thanks a lot, Saad.

00:06:39.572 --> 00:06:40.280
Great to be here.

00:06:40.280 --> 00:06:43.630
[MUSIC PLAYING]

